                        <img src="/images/projects/advanced-materials/tensile-machine.jpg" alt="ZwickRoell mechanical testing machine with grips and a printed polymer specimen mounted for testing" width="804" height="1072" />
                    </a>
                    <figcaption><strong>Mechanical testing setup</strong><span>Original photograph from the tensile laboratory report</span></figcaption>
                </figure>
            </section>

            <nav class="page-index" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#fabrication">Fabrication</a><a href="#mechanical">Mechanical tests</a><a href="#thermal">Thermal analysis</a><a href="#structure">Structure &amp; imaging</a><a href="#planning">Test planning</a></nav>

            <section class="section" id="overview">
                <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">01</p><h2>Connecting Process to Properties</h2></div><p>The experiments examined different materials and specimen sets: carbon-fiber-reinforced PETG, PLA, and a carbon sample for diffraction analysis.</p></div>
                <div class="results-grid">
                    <article><strong>13 min</strong><h3>Shorter print batch</h3><p>Three-specimen CF-PETG batch: 77 min at 100% infill and 64 min at 20% infill.</p></article>
                    <article><strong>71.42 MPa</strong><h3>Mean flexural strength</h3><p>Three CF-PETG specimens at 80% infill, tested in three-point bending.</p></article>
                    <article><strong>74.8 °C</strong><h3>DMA tan δ peak</h3><p>Glass-transition estimate using the tan δ peak criterion for the tested PLA sample.</p></article>
                </div>
                <p class="media-note">Results describe the specimens and conditions in these course experiments.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="section dark" id="contributions">
                <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">02</p><h2>My Work</h2></div><p>My coursework connected specimen preparation, instrument data, and individual engineering reports.</p></div>
                <div class="contribution-list">
                    <div><span>01</span><p>Worked through specimen CAD in Fusion 360, slicing in PrusaSlicer, and FDM print preparation while documenting processing conditions.</p></div>
                    <div><span>02</span><p>Analyzed mechanical and thermal test data, plotted response curves, and summarized specimen-level results in laboratory reports.</p></div>
                    <div><span>03</span><p>Compared diffraction peaks with a reference pattern, calculated interplanar spacing, and examined PLA fibers through optical microscopy.</p></div>
                    <div><span>04</span><p>Developed a proposed torsion experiment to compare two polymers across three infill levels.</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section" id="fabrication">
                <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">03</p><h2>Fabricating the Specimens</h2></div><p>A controlled print series varied infill from 20% to 100% while holding specimen dimensions and raster angle constant.</p></div>
                <div class="split">
                    <div class="copy">
                        <h3>CAD → slicing → FDM printing</h3>
                        <p>The CF-PETG specimens measured 125 × 12.7 × 3.2 mm. The workflow used Fusion 360, PrusaSlicer, and an enclosed Hydra 21 printer, with filament drying, bed leveling, Z-offset adjustment, and first-layer monitoring.</p>
                        <dl class="settings"><div><dt>Raster angle</dt><dd>0°</dd></div><div><dt>Nozzle / bed</dt><dd>245 / 60 °C</dd></div><div><dt>Print speed</dt><dd>30 mm/s</dd></div><div><dt>Batch size</dt><dd>3 specimens</dd></div></dl>
                        <p>Reducing infill from 100% to 20% shortened the recorded batch time by 13 minutes, about 17%. This experiment measured print time; it did not establish an optimum infill for strength.</p>
                    </div>
                    <figure class="media-card chart">
                        <a href="/images/projects/advanced-materials/infill-print-time.svg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open full print time chart"><img src="/images/projects/advanced-materials/infill-print-time.svg" alt="Three-specimen batch print times at 20, 40, 60, 80, and 100 percent infill: 64, 67, 70, 73, and 77 minutes" loading="lazy" /></a>
                        <figcaption><strong>Infill and batch print time</strong><span>Replotted from Lab 1 Data.xlsx. Each time represents three specimens printed together.</span></figcaption>
                    </figure>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section" id="mechanical">
                <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">04</p><h2>Mechanical Testing</h2></div><p>Three-point bending quantified the flexural response of printed CF-PETG. A separate tensile lab examined dogbone specimens.</p></div>
                <div class="split mechanical-results">
                    <div class="copy">
                        <h3>Three-point bending</h3>
                        <p>Three 80%-infill specimens were tested using a ZwickRoell 20 kN machine, a 56 mm support span, and a reported crosshead rate of 0.5 mm/min.</p>
                        <table>
                            <caption>CF-PETG flexural results · n = 3</caption>
                            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Specimen</th><th scope="col">Strength<br />(MPa)</th><th scope="col">Modulus<br />(GPa)</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody><tr><th scope="row">1</th><td>71.78</td><td>2.621</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2</th><td>71.30</td><td>2.507</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">3</th><td>71.17</td><td>2.612</td></tr><tr class="mean"><th scope="row">Mean</th><td>71.42</td><td>2.580</td></tr></tbody>
                        </table>
                        <p class="media-note">Means checked against the instrument export. The three strength values span 71.17–71.78 MPa.</p>
                    </div>
                    <figure class="media-card chart">
                        <a href="/images/projects/advanced-materials/flexural-stress-strain.svg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open full flexural stress strain chart"><img src="/images/projects/advanced-materials/flexural-stress-strain.svg" alt="Flexural stress versus strain for three CF-PETG specimens with closely grouped peaks near 71 MPa at about 4 percent strain" loading="lazy" /></a>
                        <figcaption><strong>Flexural stress–strain response</strong><span>Three specimen traces replotted from the original Lab 2 instrument export.</span></figcaption>
                    </figure>
                </div>
                <div class="specimen-grid">
                    <figure class="media-card specimen">
                        <a href="/images/projects/advanced-materials/flexure-specimen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open full photograph of the flexure specimen"><img src="/images/projects/advanced-materials/flexure-specimen.jpg" alt="Rectangular black CF-PETG flexure specimen with its print settings labeled at one end" width="1431" height="1073" loading="lazy" /></a>
                        <figcaption><strong>Flexural specimen</strong><span>Rectangular CF-PETG specimen from the bending lab.</span></figcaption>
                    </figure>
                    <figure class="media-card specimen">
                        <a href="/images/projects/advanced-materials/printed-dogbones.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open full photograph of the tensile specimens"><img src="/images/projects/advanced-materials/printed-dogbones.jpg" alt="Two printed CF-PETG dogbone specimens photographed before tensile testing" width="1366" height="1822" loading="lazy" /></a>
                        <figcaption><strong>Separate tensile experiment</strong><span>Dogbone specimens tested with a reported 57 mm gauge length and 5 mm/min crosshead rate.</span></figcaption>
                    </figure>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section" id="thermal">
                <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">05</p><h2>Thermal Analysis of PLA</h2></div><p>Three methods examined different responses: temperature-dependent stiffness, mass loss, and heat flow.</p></div>
                <div class="thermal-layout">
                    <article class="thermal-study dma-study">
                        <div><p class="eyebrow">Dynamic Mechanical Analysis</p><h3>Stiffness changes with temperature</h3>
                        <p>A TA Q800 test tracked storage modulus, loss modulus, and tan δ. The tan δ peak occurred at 74.8 °C. This is the glass-transition estimate for this test using that criterion.</p>
                        </div>
                        <figure class="media-card chart"><a href="/images/projects/advanced-materials/pla-dma.svg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open full PLA DMA chart"><img src="/images/projects/advanced-materials/pla-dma.svg" alt="PLA DMA curves from 30 to 120 degrees Celsius: storage modulus drops sharply through the transition region and tan delta peaks at 74.8 degrees Celsius" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>DMA: modulus and damping</strong><span>Replotted from the Lab 3 export, with moduli converted from MPa to GPa.</span></figcaption></figure>
                    </article>
                    <div class="thermal-stack">
                        <article class="thermal-study">
                            <p class="eyebrow">Thermogravimetric Analysis</p><h3>Mass loss during heating</h3>
                            <p>A Q500 run heated PLA under nitrogen at a programmed 10 °C/min. The recorded trace shows about 99.6% mass loss by 584 °C, with the main decrease around 350–415 °C.</p>
                            <figure class="media-card chart"><a href="/images/projects/advanced-materials/pla-tga.svg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open full PLA TGA chart"><img src="/images/projects/advanced-materials/pla-tga.svg" alt="PLA mass remaining versus temperature: most mass is lost around 350 to 415 degrees Celsius, leaving 0.38 percent at 584 degrees Celsius" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>TGA: mass remaining</strong><span>Normalized to the first recorded mass, 20.88686 mg.</span></figcaption></figure>
                        </article>
                        <article class="thermal-study">
                            <p class="eyebrow">Differential Scanning Calorimetry</p><h3>Crystallization and melting features</h3>
                            <p>The PLA heating trace showed a crystallization feature near 105 °C and a melting feature near 170 °C. These approximate temperatures describe this recorded run.</p>
                            <figure class="media-card chart"><a href="/images/projects/advanced-materials/pla-dsc-heating.svg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open full PLA DSC heating chart"><img src="/images/projects/advanced-materials/pla-dsc-heating.svg" alt="PLA DSC heating trace with a crystallization feature around 105 degrees Celsius and a melting feature around 170 degrees Celsius" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>DSC: heating portion of the run</strong><span>Replotted from Neat PLA.xlsx. Heat-flow values retain the export's unspecified units.</span></figcaption></figure>
                        </article>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section" id="structure">
                <div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">06</p><h2>Structure &amp; Imaging</h2></div><p>Microscopy and diffraction extended the work beyond bulk mechanical and thermal response.</p></div>
                <div class="imaging-grid">
                    <figure class="media-card"><a href="/images/projects/advanced-materials/pla-fibers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open full optical microscopy image of PLA fibers"><img src="/images/projects/advanced-materials/pla-fibers.jpg" alt="Optical microscopy image showing overlapping PLA fibers and their visible arrangement" width="1114" height="835" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>PLA fiber microscopy</strong><span>Examining visible fiber morphology and arrangement.</span></figcaption></figure>
                    <figure class="media-card"><a href="/images/projects/advanced-materials/pla-fibers-low-light.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open full low illumination microscopy image"><img src="/images/projects/advanced-materials/pla-fibers-low-light.jpg" alt="Low illumination microscopy view of overlapping PLA fibers, with dark outlines against a gray background" width="1600" height="1200" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Low-illumination view</strong><span>A second image from the optical microscopy lab.</span></figcaption></figure>
                </div>
                <div class="split xrd-study">
                    <div class="copy"><h3>X-ray diffraction analysis</h3><p>The carbon-sample diffraction pattern was compared with a graphite reference. The analysis used the reference peak positions and Bragg's law to calculate interplanar spacing.</p><p>This exercise focused on peak comparison and spacing calculations. The images alone do not establish a quantitative purity or defect concentration.</p></div>
                    <figure class="media-card chart"><a href="/images/projects/advanced-materials/carbon-xrd.png" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open full original carbon sample diffraction plot"><img src="/images/projects/advanced-materials/carbon-xrd.png" alt="Original carbon sample X-ray diffraction plot with a prominent peak near 26 degrees two theta and smaller peaks at higher angles" width="928" height="426" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Original sample diffraction plot</strong><span>From the Lab 6 report. Select the image to inspect the original plot.</span></figcaption></figure>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="section dark split" id="planning">
                <div><p class="eyebrow">07</p><h2>Planning the Next Experiment</h2></div>
                <div class="copy"><p class="lead">A proposed torsion study: two materials × three infill levels.</p><p>The design of experiments paired PLA and PETG with 20%, 60%, and 100% infill, giving six planned test conditions. The worksheet held specimen length at 165 mm and thickness at 3 mm.</p><div class="doe-grid"><div><strong>PLA</strong><span>20% · 60% · 100% infill</span></div><div><strong>PETG</strong><span>20% · 60% · 100% infill</span></div></div><p>Planned outputs included angle of twist, maximum torque, shear modulus, and torsional strength. This work stopped at the test plan; the worksheet contains no measured torsion results.</p><h3>Engineering Takeaway</h3><p>A useful material result needs a defined specimen, processing history, loading mode, and test conditions. Across these labs, I practiced connecting those details to the data and documenting the limits of each conclusion.</p></div>
            </section>

